<TopicPage slug="subjects/physics/intermediate/thermal-physics">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Temperature is not heat. <strong>Temperature</strong> measures the average jiggle energy of
    molecules; <strong>heat</strong> is energy in transit because of a temperature difference.
    Squeeze a gas into half the volume at fixed temperature and the pressure <strong>doubles</strong>
    (Boyle's law) — because molecules now strike the walls twice as often. Three gas laws plus
    energy conservation make thermal physics.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="Boyle's law at three temperatures — PV = nRT drawn as hyperbolas; hotter gas, higher curve" widget="plotter" config={isothermCfg} />

  <p>
    Each hyperbola is one mole of ideal gas at a fixed temperature (P = RT/V, R = 8.314 J/mol·K).
    Compressing along any curve raises pressure; jumping curves (heating at fixed volume) raises
    it too. Real engines live on exactly these diagrams.
  </p>

  <h2>The laws, ranked by audacity</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Zeroth law:</strong> things in contact reach one temperature — this is what makes thermometers meaningful at all.</li>
    <li><strong>First law:</strong> energy conservation with a name change — heat added = internal energy gained + work done. No engine creates energy.</li>
    <li><strong>Second law:</strong> heat flows hot → cold, never back, for free. Entropy (disorder-accounting) increases in any closed system — the only fundamental law with an arrow of time.</li>
    <li><strong>Third law:</strong> absolute zero (−273.15 °C) is unreachable — where molecular jiggle is minimal.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A quantum of intuition: at room temperature a nitrogen molecule travels ~500 m/s and collides
    billions of times per second. Temperature is simply the statistical bookkeeping of all that
    microscopic chaos — statistical mechanics (the advanced rung) makes the counting precise.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> OpenStax, College Physics (Temperature, Kinetic Theory); Feynman Lectures Vol. I, ch. 39–41.</p>
</TopicPage>
